questions.corrige_markdown:
**(i) The Douala ruler (1 mark).** A prominent ruler of the **Douala chiefs of the Akwa and Bell families**. Textbooks usually give **King Akwa** (the Akwa family of Douala) and, in some books, **Prince Rudolf Douala Manga Bell**, with the support of educated Douala such as the clerk **Ngoso Din**, who sent complaints against Puttkamer to the Reichstag in Berlin. Either of the two rulers is accepted.

**(ii) A ruthless policy (1 mark).** Any one of:
- **forced labour**, in which Africans were recruited by force for the plantations, the railways and road building;
- **flogging** with the hippopotamus-hide whip as a punishment;
- **heavy taxation** and the seizure of land for plantations and concession companies;
- **punitive expeditions** against peoples who resisted.

**(iii) The replacement (1 mark).** **Theodor Seitz**, Governor from 1907 to 1910.

**(iv) One reason the natives favoured the new Governor (1 mark).** Seitz was regarded as **milder and more moderate** than Puttkamer: he reduced the worst abuses (brutality and forced labour), was ready to listen to the complaints of chiefs and gave more attention to roads, schools and health.
